          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            To some extent,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            are a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            %em semiclassical approximation
            or
            %em quasiclassical approximation
            to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is the restriction of this deformation to just first order (or some finite order) in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} semiclassical approximation
                  %th …
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    order of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℏ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^0)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^1)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^n)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^\infty)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+state"} semiclassical state
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+observable"} classical observable
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observable"} quantum observable
          %p
            Applied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Fields
                  %mi D
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi iS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{\phi \in \mathbf{Fields}} D\phi\; F(\phi) e^{iS(\phi)/\hbar}
            by an expansion in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+points"} critical points
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (hence the solutions of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  rotating wave approximation
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/rotating+wave+approximation"} ?
            = succeed "-space" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            varies fast as measured in units of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex phases
            of the integrand
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S / \hbar )
            tend to cancel each other in the integral so that substantial contributions to the integral come only from the vicininity of critical points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (classical
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
          %p
            But semiclassical approximations can be applied to most other formulations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
            Notably in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            of quantum evolution, solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \hbar \frac{d}{d t} \psi = \hat H \psi
            (which characterizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+functions"} wave functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian dynamics
            induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton+operator"} Hamilton operator
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat H
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WKB+method"} WKB method
            or similar, amounts to expressing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function"} wave function
            in the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi = exp(S)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a slowly varying function and solving the equation for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr-Sommerfeld+leaf"} Bohr-Sommerfeld quantization conditions
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+state"} semiclassical state
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WKB+method"} WKB method
            makes sense for a more general class of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+equations"} wave equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+mechanics"} wave
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optics"} optics
            this yields the short-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavelength"} wavelength
            limit of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometrical+optics"} geometrical optics
            approximation. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %span.newWikiWord
                  eikonal
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/eikonal"} ?
          %p
            Multidimensional generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WKB+method"} WKB method
            appear to be rather nontrivial; they have been pioneered by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Maslov"} Victor Maslov
            who introduced a topological invariant to remove ambiguities of the naive version of the method, called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maslov+index"} Maslov index
          %h2#related_phenomena Related phenomena
          %h3#equivariant_localization Equivariant localization
          %p In some special cases (most often in the presence of supersymmetry) the main contribution (the first term in expansion) amounts to the true result; the quantum correction sometimes leads however to an overall scalar factor. This is the case of so-called localization (related directly in some cases to the equivariant localization in cohomology and Lefshetz-type fixed point formulas). Most of well known examples of integrable systems and TQFTs lead to localization.
          %h3#large_limit_in_gauge_theories
            Large
            = succeed "-limit" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            of gauge theories, which is of importance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collective+field+theory"} collective field theory
            and in the study of relation between gauge and string theories is formally very similar to semiclassical expansion, where the role of Planck constant is played by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi N
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/N^2
          %h3#in_radiation_theory In radiation theory
          %p In the theory of radiation there is a different meaning of semiclassical treatment: one considers particles in a sorrounding electromagnetic field and the particles are treated as in finite-dimensional quantum mechanics, with the electromagnetic field as an external classical field coupled to the particles via an interaction term.
